Theorem 2.5.6 Let X be either a Q-homology plane with ML (X) = k or
a Platonic A1 -fiber space possibly except for the case where the multiplicity
sequence is {2, 2, m} with m > 5. Then there are no étale morphisms from
X to A2 .

Proof. We first treat the case where X is a Q-homology plane with ML(X) =
k. Let f : X → A2 be an étale morphism which we do not have to assume
to be almost surjective. Let p : S → X be the universal covering, where S
is an affine hypersurface xy = z n − 1 in A3 . We refer to [29] for relevant
results. Let ϕ = f · p. Then ϕ : S → A2 is an étale morphism. We show
that such ϕ does not exist. Note that the canonical divisor KS is trivial
and Γ(OS )∗ = k ∗ . By a straightforward computation, one can show that
a differential 2-form ω = (1/z n−1 )dx ∧ dy is an everywhere nonzero regular
form. On the other hand, since ϕ is étale, there exist elements f, g of Γ(OS )
such that ϕ∗ (df ∧ dg) is an everywhere nonzero regular 2-form. Hence we
2.5. AFFINE PSEUDO-COVERINGS                                                  117

have ω = cϕ∗ (df ∧ dg). Meanwhile, there exists a free Z/nZ-action given
by (x, y, z) 7→ (ζx, ζ −1y, ζ dz), where ζ is a primitive n-th root of unity with
n = |G| and 0 < d < n. Then ζ(ω) = ζ −d(n−1) ω and ϕ∗ (df ∧ dg) is invariant
under this Z/nZ-action because ϕ splits via X. So, d(n − 1) is divisible by
n, which is not the case.

